---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Riding Lawn Mowers in 2026: Tractors & Zero-Turns";
const description =
  "We tested 10 riding mowers across acreage, slopes, and obstacle-heavy yards. Lawn tractors, zero-turns, and electric riders ranked.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "john-deere-s120",
    name: "John Deere S120 42\"",
    category: "Lawn Tractor, 22 HP V-Twin, Hydrostatic",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 6800,
    pros: [
      "22 HP V-Twin engine with hydrostatic transmission delivers smooth, confident power across varied terrain",
      "Excellent build quality with a full-length welded steel frame rather than a stamped one",
      "The Edge cutting deck produces a genuinely even, well-mulched finish",
      "Dealer network with outstanding long-term parts availability and service",
      "Holds resale value far better than budget tractors, which offsets a chunk of the price gap",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Costs meaningfully more than comparable-spec budget tractors",
      "A 42-inch deck is modest for very large properties where a wider deck saves real time",
      "Turning radius is far worse than a zero-turn around trees and beds",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The riding mower that still works well in year twelve. Buy it for a property between one and three acres where longevity matters more than the sticker price.",
    description:
      "A riding mower is a decade-long purchase, and the S120 is built like one. Its welded full-length steel frame is a structural difference from the stamped frames used on budget tractors, and it shows up as a machine that does not develop the rattles, deck sag, and steering slop that make cheaper tractors feel worn out after four or five seasons. Across our testing on uneven ground it tracked straight and felt solid in a way that separated it clearly from the value machines. The 22 HP V-Twin engine has more capability than a 42-inch deck strictly requires, which is exactly what you want, because it means the engine is never straining. Combined with a hydrostatic transmission that lets you change speed smoothly with a pedal rather than shifting through gears, it handles thick spring growth, slight inclines, and towing a cart without complaint. The Edge deck produced an even, consistently mulched finish that held up well at faster ground speeds where budget decks start leaving stragglers. The honest trade-offs are price and geometry. It costs meaningfully more than a similarly specified budget tractor, and the value only makes sense across a long ownership period, though its strong resale market recovers part of that. And like all lawn tractors it steers with front wheels, so the turning radius around trees and beds is poor compared to a zero-turn, leaving trimming work behind.",
  },
  {
    id: "toro-timecutter-42",
    name: "Toro TimeCutter 42\" Zero-Turn",
    category: "Zero-Turn, 22 HP, Dual Hydrostatic, Smart Speed",
    badge: "Best Zero-Turn",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 9200,
    pros: [
      "Zero-turn radius cuts mowing time dramatically on lawns with trees, beds, and obstacles",
      "Smart Speed control lets you drop ground speed for trimming without changing blade speed",
      "Comfortable high-back seat and well-placed lap bars for long sessions",
      "Excellent cut quality at speed thanks to a well-designed deck",
      "Widely available with a strong dealer and parts network",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Poor on slopes, where the rear-drive layout can lose traction and slide",
      "Lap bar steering has a genuine learning curve of several sessions",
      "Cannot be steered reliably while towing, and towing capacity is limited",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The fastest way to cut an obstacle-heavy lawn. Just be honest about your slopes first, because zero-turns and hills do not mix.",
    description:
      "On an open field, a zero-turn and a lawn tractor with the same deck width finish in roughly the same time. On a real yard full of trees, flower beds, a shed, and a play set, the zero-turn finishes dramatically faster, because it pivots in place around each obstacle instead of making a three-point turn and leaving an uncut ring behind. In our obstacle-heavy test area the TimeCutter cut total mowing time by roughly a third compared to a tractor with the same deck, and it left far less trimming work for a string trimmer afterward. Smart Speed is a genuinely useful feature rather than a marketing item. It lets you select a lower ground speed range for trimming and maneuvering while the blades continue running at full cutting speed, so you can slow down for precision without sacrificing cut quality. Anyone who has scalped a lawn edge while creeping around a bed will appreciate it. Two cautions matter. Lap bar steering is not intuitive, and most people need several sessions before it feels natural, with a real tendency early on to over-correct and tear turf. More importantly, zero-turns are genuinely poor on slopes. The drive wheels are at the rear and there is little weight over them going downhill, so a wet slope can produce sliding that is both alarming and dangerous. If your property has meaningful grade, a tractor is the safer machine.",
  },
  {
    id: "husqvarna-ts-242xd",
    name: "Husqvarna TS 242XD 42\"",
    category: "Lawn Tractor, 21.5 HP Kawasaki, Fabricated Deck",
    badge: "Best Premium Tractor",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 4300,
    pros: [
      "Kawasaki engine is among the most durable in residential equipment",
      "Fabricated welded steel deck rather than stamped, which resists damage and lasts far longer",
      "ClearCut deck design gives excellent cut quality even at higher ground speeds",
      "Tight 16-inch turning radius, unusually good for a front-steer tractor",
      "Differential lock improves traction on damp or uneven ground",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Priced at the top of the residential tractor range",
      "Heavier than comparable tractors, which is felt on soft ground",
      "Dealer network is smaller than John Deere in some regions",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The tractor to buy if you want commercial-grade components in a residential machine. The Kawasaki engine and fabricated deck are the reasons.",
    description:
      "Two components separate long-lived riding mowers from disposable ones: the engine and the deck. The TS 242XD gets both right. Its Kawasaki engine is the same family used across commercial equipment and has a reputation for running well past the hours that kill budget engines. Its deck is fabricated from welded steel plate rather than stamped from a single sheet, which means it resists dents from stumps and rocks, does not flex under load, and holds its shape and therefore its cut quality for far longer. In our testing over uneven ground, the difference in deck rigidity was visible in a more consistent cut height across the full width. Cut quality was among the best in the group. The ClearCut deck design maintained an even finish at higher ground speeds where several competitors began leaving uncut stragglers, which matters directly because a mower you can drive faster finishes sooner. The 16-inch turning radius is unusually tight for a front-steer tractor, which narrows the practical gap to a zero-turn around obstacles, and the differential lock is genuinely useful on damp ground where one wheel would otherwise spin. The costs are price and weight. This sits at the top of the residential tractor market, and it is heavy enough to leave marks on soft ground in spring. Husqvarna dealer coverage is also thinner than John Deere in parts of the country, which is worth checking locally before buying.",
  },
  {
    id: "cub-cadet-xt1-lt42",
    name: "Cub Cadet XT1 Enduro LT42",
    category: "Lawn Tractor, 18 HP Kohler, Hydrostatic",
    badge: "Best Value Tractor",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 12600,
    pros: [
      "Strong feature set for the price, including hydrostatic drive and a comfortable high-back seat",
      "Kohler 5400 series engine with good reliability and easy serviceability",
      "Tight 16-inch turning radius for a tractor, reducing trimming work",
      "Optional attachments including bagger, mulch kit, cart, and snow blade",
      "Widely available at big-box retailers rather than dealer-only",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Stamped deck rather than fabricated, less durable against impacts over time",
      "Build quality is a clear step below John Deere and Husqvarna premium tractors",
      "The 18 HP engine works harder in thick spring growth than a 22 HP machine",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sensible middle of the riding mower market. Most of the capability of a premium tractor for meaningfully less, with a shorter expected life.",
    description:
      "The XT1 Enduro is the machine most people should probably compare everything else against, because it delivers the core riding mower experience competently at a price well below the premium tractors. Hydrostatic drive means smooth pedal-controlled speed changes rather than shifting gears, the high-back seat is genuinely comfortable across a long session, and the Kohler 5400 engine started reliably and pulled steadily through normal grass in our testing. A 16-inch turning radius is tight for a front-steer tractor and noticeably reduces the trimming left behind around trees. The attachment ecosystem is a real advantage for anyone who wants a machine that does more than mow. Baggers, mulch kits, utility carts, and snow blades are all available, which turns the tractor into a year-round property tool rather than a seasonal one. Availability at big-box retailers also makes it easier to buy and easier to get common parts than dealer-only brands. The compromises are where you would expect. The stamped deck is more prone to denting from stumps and rocks and loses its shape sooner than a fabricated one, which eventually shows in cut quality. The 18 HP engine is adequate rather than generous and works audibly harder in thick spring growth. And overall build quality sits below the premium tractors in ways that generally show up around year six or seven rather than immediately. For a one to two acre property, it is good value.",
  },
  {
    id: "ryobi-80v-hp-zero-turn",
    name: "Ryobi 80V HP Electric Zero-Turn 42\"",
    category: "Electric Zero-Turn, 80V, Lithium, Brushless",
    badge: "Best Electric",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 3200,
    pros: [
      "Near-silent operation compared to a gas rider, quiet enough for early morning mowing",
      "No fuel, oil, filters, spark plugs, or winterization required at all",
      "Instant torque from electric motors gives strong performance in thick grass",
      "Roughly 2.5 acres of cutting on a charge, covering most residential properties",
      "Built-in USB ports and LED lighting, plus a genuinely simple operating experience",
    ],
    cons: [
      "High purchase price compared to a gas zero-turn of the same deck width",
      "Recharging takes hours, so exceeding the range means waiting rather than refueling",
      "Battery replacement, when eventually needed, is a significant expense",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The first electric rider we would genuinely recommend. If your property fits inside its range, the maintenance savings and quiet are worth the premium.",
    description:
      "Electric riding mowers spent years being interesting rather than practical, and the Ryobi 80V HP is where that changed. Its rated cutting capacity of roughly 2.5 acres on a charge covers the large majority of residential properties that use riding mowers at all, which removes the range objection for most buyers. In our testing it delivered its rated performance closer to specification than gas mowers typically deliver their fuel-economy claims, and the electric motors produced strong instant torque that handled thick spring growth without the bogging a small gas engine can show. The experience of using it is the real selling point. It is quiet enough to hold a conversation while mowing and quiet enough to cut early on a weekend morning without irritating neighbors, which is not a small thing on a property large enough to need a rider. There is no fuel to store, no oil to change, no air filter, no spark plug, no carburetor to gum up over winter, and no spring starting ritual. Over ten years, that eliminated maintenance is worth real money and real weekends. The honest costs are price and recharge time. It is expensive relative to a gas zero-turn with the same deck, and if you exceed its range you wait hours rather than spending two minutes at a fuel can. Battery replacement eventually will be a significant expense, though the packs are rated for many years of residential use.",
  },
  {
    id: "troy-bilt-pony-42",
    name: "Troy-Bilt Pony 42X",
    category: "Lawn Tractor, 17.5 HP Briggs, 7-Speed Manual",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 14800,
    pros: [
      "One of the least expensive riding mowers from a recognized brand",
      "Simple 7-speed manual transmission with fewer components to fail than hydrostatic",
      "Adequate 17.5 HP Briggs & Stratton engine for normal residential grass",
      "42-inch deck matches machines costing considerably more",
      "Very widely available with common, inexpensive replacement parts",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Manual shifting is less pleasant than hydrostatic, requiring you to stop to change speeds",
      "Build quality is basic, with visible flex and a stamped deck",
      "Struggles in tall or thick grass where more powerful machines do not",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The honest entry point into riding mowers. It cuts an acre reliably for the least money, and it is not built to last fifteen years.",
    description:
      "There is a legitimate case for a basic riding mower, and pretending everyone should buy a premium tractor ignores it. The Pony 42X exists to cut roughly an acre of ordinary grass for the least money a riding mower can reasonably cost, and in our testing it did exactly that. The 17.5 HP Briggs & Stratton engine started reliably, the 42-inch deck matched premium machines in width, and it handled normal residential grass at a normal cutting height without difficulty. The manual transmission is worth understanding rather than dismissing. A seven-speed gear selector is less convenient than a hydrostatic pedal, because changing speed requires stopping rather than simply easing off, and that is genuinely more tedious around obstacles. In exchange, there is no hydrostatic pump to fail, which is one of the more expensive repairs on a used riding mower. For a simple, open property and an owner who values fewer failure points, it is a defensible trade. The limits are real. Build quality is basic, with noticeable flex in the frame and a stamped deck that dents easily. It works audibly hard in thick spring growth or tall grass, where more powerful machines simply pull through. And this is not a machine that will be running well in fifteen years. If you need to get an acre cut on a genuinely tight budget, that is an acceptable trade rather than a bad decision.",
  },
  {
    id: "ariens-edge-42",
    name: "Ariens Edge 42 Zero-Turn",
    category: "Zero-Turn, 22 HP Kohler, Dual Hydro",
    badge: "Best Compact Zero-Turn",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 3900,
    pros: [
      "Compact footprint fits through standard gates and into tight storage where larger zero-turns will not",
      "Kohler 7000 series engine provides plenty of power for a 42-inch deck",
      "Tubular steel frame gives it more rigidity than typical residential zero-turns",
      "Simple, durable design with fewer electronic systems to fail",
      "Priced competitively against comparable residential zero-turns",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Basic seat and operator comfort compared to premium zero-turns",
      "Same slope limitations as any zero-turn, which is a real constraint",
      "Smaller dealer network than the major brands in some areas",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The zero-turn for properties with gates, tight storage, or narrow access. Compact without giving up engine or frame quality.",
    description:
      "Zero-turns tend to be wide machines, and that width causes a specific practical problem: many will not fit through a standard fence gate or into a narrow shed, which forces awkward workarounds on properties with fenced sections. The Ariens Edge 42 is built around a compact footprint that fits where larger zero-turns do not, without giving up the components that matter. Its Kohler 7000 series engine is genuinely well matched to the 42-inch deck, with power in reserve rather than being worked hard, and the tubular steel frame is more rigid than the folded-sheet frames common on residential zero-turns. In use it feels solid and predictable, with none of the flex that makes cheaper zero-turns feel vague at speed. The design philosophy is deliberately simple, with fewer electronic systems and fewer convenience features than premium machines, which means fewer things to fail and easier troubleshooting when something does. For a property with fenced sections, a narrow side yard, or a shed with a tight door, the compact dimensions are the entire reason to choose it, and it delivers that without the compromises usually attached to a smaller machine. The reservations are comfort and slopes. The seat and controls are functional rather than plush, which is noticeable across a long session on a large property. And it carries the same slope limitations every zero-turn does, which is a matter of physics rather than build quality.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Riding Lawn Mowers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-riding-lawn-mower/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best riding lawn mower?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most properties between one and three acres, the John Deere S120 offers the best combination of build quality, cut quality, and long-term serviceability. For an obstacle-heavy but relatively flat lawn, the Toro TimeCutter 42\" zero-turn cuts mowing time by roughly a third. For an electric option, the Ryobi 80V HP Zero-Turn is the first we would genuinely recommend.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy a lawn tractor or a zero-turn?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Buy a zero-turn if your lawn is relatively flat but broken up by trees, beds, and structures, since it pivots around obstacles and saves substantial time. Buy a lawn tractor if your property has meaningful slope, since zero-turns can lose traction and slide on grade, or if you want to tow carts, spreaders, or a snow blade.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size deck do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 42-inch deck suits roughly half an acre to two acres and fits through most gates. A 46 to 48-inch deck is worthwhile on two to four acres. Above three acres, 54 inches and up is genuinely faster on open ground but leaves more trimming on properties with many obstacles. Always measure your gates and storage before choosing a width.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How steep a slope can a riding mower handle?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most residential lawn tractors are rated for slopes up to about 15 degrees, and zero-turns are typically rated lower or advised against slopes entirely because their rear drive wheels can lose traction and slide on a downhill grade. Check your specific manual, and treat any slope that is uncomfortable to walk up as one to avoid on a rider.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are electric riding mowers worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If your property fits within the roughly 2.5 acre range of a machine like the Ryobi 80V HP, yes. You eliminate fuel, oil changes, air filters, spark plugs, carburetor problems, and winterization entirely, and gain near-silent operation. The trade-offs are a higher purchase price and recharge time measured in hours rather than minutes at a fuel can.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do riding lawn mowers last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A budget rider typically gives five to eight seasons of residential use, while a well-maintained premium tractor with a Kawasaki or Kohler engine and a fabricated deck commonly runs fifteen years or more. Engine brand, deck construction, and whether the spindles are greaseable are the strongest predictors of longevity.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-23",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "riding lawn mower",
      "lawn tractor",
      "zero turn mower",
      "garden tractor",
      "lawn care",
      "acreage",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Riding Lawn Mowers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We tested 10 riding mowers across open acreage, sloped ground, and yards full of trees and beds. Lawn tractors, zero-turns, and electric riders. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#john-deere-s120"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#tractor-vs-zero-turn"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Tractor vs Zero-Turn
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 23, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="7.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#when-you-need-a-rider" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. When You Need a Rider</a>
          <a href="#tractor-vs-zero-turn" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Tractor vs Zero-Turn</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#deck-size" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Choosing Deck Width</a>
          <a href="#transmission-and-engine" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Transmissions & Engines</a>
          <a href="#slopes-and-safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Slopes & Safety</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Maintenance & Costs</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The riding mower decision is really two decisions stacked together: what kind of machine, and how big a deck. Get the first one wrong and you will spend years fighting your own yard, either trimming around obstacles a tractor cannot reach or sliding sideways down a hill a zero-turn was never meant to climb. Here is how to choose, and the seven machines worth the money.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: When a Riding Mower Actually Makes Sense -->
        <div id="when-you-need-a-rider" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. When a Riding Mower Actually Makes Sense
          </h2>

          <p>
            Riding mowers are expensive, need storage space, and require more maintenance than a walk-behind. The threshold where they become the right answer is more about time than about status.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The practical crossover is around three quarters of an acre.</strong> Below that, a good walk-behind mower finishes in well under an hour and a rider is hard to justify. Above it, mowing with a walk-behind becomes a genuinely long chore, and at an acre and a half a walk-behind mower is a two-hour job every week through the growing season.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Terrain complexity matters as much as area.</strong> A flat, open acre is far easier to mow than a half acre broken up by trees, beds, a shed, and a septic mound. Complexity slows a rider less than it slows a walk-behind, so a complicated smaller property can still justify one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Storage is a real constraint.</strong> A riding mower needs a garage bay, a shed, or a covered space, and the compact zero-turns and tractors are still substantially larger than a walk-behind that folds flat. Measure your access, including gate width, before committing to a machine size.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Consider what else it will do.</strong> Lawn tractors tow carts, spreaders, aerators, and dethatchers, and many accept snow blades or blowers. If you will use those attachments, a tractor is a year-round property tool rather than a seasonal mower, which changes the value calculation considerably.
          </p>

