        <!-- SECTION 2: Lawn Tractor vs Zero-Turn -->
        <div id="tractor-vs-zero-turn" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Lawn Tractor vs Zero-Turn
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the decision that determines whether you enjoy your mower for the next decade, and it comes down to two factors: how many obstacles you have, and how steep your ground is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lawn Tractors</h3>

          <p>
            A lawn tractor steers with its front wheels like a car and is driven from a conventional seat with a steering wheel. It handles slopes far better than a zero-turn because the weight distribution and front-wheel steering keep it stable and predictable on grade. It tows well, accepts a wide range of attachments, and is intuitive to operate from the first minute. The disadvantage is turning radius: even a good tractor leaves an uncut ring around every tree and a strip along every bed edge, which becomes trimming work later.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Zero-Turn Mowers</h3>

          <p>
            A zero-turn drives each rear wheel independently through separate hydrostatic transmissions, controlled by two lap bars. Pushing one bar forward and pulling the other pivots the machine in place, letting it turn around a tree without leaving anything uncut. On an obstacle-heavy lawn this cuts mowing time by roughly a third and dramatically reduces trimming. The disadvantages are slopes, where zero-turns can lose traction and slide, limited towing ability, and a genuine learning curve on the controls.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            Walk your property. If it is largely open with meaningful slope, buy a tractor. If it is broken up by trees, beds, and structures but relatively flat, buy a zero-turn. If it has both obstacles and real slope, buy a tractor, because a mower you cannot operate safely on a hill is worse than one that leaves some trimming.
          </p>

