        <!-- SECTION 4: Choosing Deck Width -->
        <div id="deck-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Choosing Deck Width
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck width is the specification that most directly determines how long mowing takes, and bigger is not automatically better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">42 Inches</h3>

          <p>
            The residential standard and the right choice for roughly half an acre to two acres. It fits through most gates, stores in a normal garage bay, and maneuvers well around obstacles. On anything above two acres it starts to feel slow, since total passes scale directly with deck width.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">46 to 48 Inches</h3>

          <p>
            A meaningful time saving on two to four acres, cutting roughly ten percent fewer passes than a 42-inch deck. Check gate widths and storage clearance carefully, because this is where machines start failing to fit through standard openings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">54 Inches and Up</h3>

          <p>
            Appropriate for three acres and beyond, and genuinely faster on open ground. On a property with many obstacles the wider deck becomes a liability, since it cannot get into the same spaces and leaves more trimming. Storage and transport also become real considerations at this size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Deck Construction Question</h3>

          <p>
            Independent of width, decks are either stamped from a single sheet of steel or fabricated from welded plate. Fabricated decks are heavier, thicker, resist damage from stumps and rocks far better, and hold their shape and therefore their cut quality for many more years. On a machine you intend to keep for a decade, a fabricated deck is one of the most worthwhile upgrades available.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Measure Your Gate Before You Shop</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              The single most common riding mower purchasing regret is buying a machine that will not fit through a fence gate, a shed door, or between a house and a garage. Measure every opening the mower must pass through, then compare against the machine's overall width including the deck discharge chute, not just the cutting width. A 42-inch deck can be well over 50 inches wide overall.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Transmissions, Engines, and What Fails First -->
        <div id="transmission-and-engine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Transmissions, Engines, and What Fails First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two systems account for most expensive riding mower repairs, and understanding them helps you buy a machine that will not need them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hydrostatic vs Manual Transmission</h3>

          <p>
            Hydrostatic transmissions use hydraulic fluid to transmit power, letting you change speed smoothly with a pedal and reverse without shifting. They are dramatically more pleasant to operate, particularly around obstacles where you constantly adjust speed. Manual gear transmissions are simpler and cheaper, with fewer parts to fail, but require stopping to change speed. For most buyers hydrostatic is worth the difference; for a simple open property on a budget, manual is defensible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Engine Brands Matter</h3>

          <p>
            Kawasaki and Kohler engines have significantly better reputations for longevity than budget alternatives, and on a machine you intend to keep for a decade the engine brand is one of the more reliable predictors of how many seasons you get. Horsepower matters less than most buyers think, provided the engine is comfortably matched to the deck: an engine with reserve capacity is never straining and lasts longer than a smaller one worked hard.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Actually Fails</h3>

          <p>
            In residential riding mowers, the most common expensive failures are hydrostatic transmission pumps, spindle bearings in the deck, and starting and charging system components. Deck spindles in particular are a wear item, and machines with greaseable spindles rather than sealed ones tend to last considerably longer because you can maintain them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Belts Are the Routine Consumable</h3>

          <p>
            Riding mowers run deck belts and drive belts that stretch and wear, and a belt failure is the single most common reason a mower stops mid-season. Keeping a spare deck belt on the shelf and knowing the routing is a small investment that prevents a week of downtime waiting on parts during peak growing season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Slopes, Stability, and Riding Mower Safety -->
        <div id="slopes-and-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Slopes, Stability, and Riding Mower Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Riding mowers are the most dangerous piece of equipment most homeowners own, and rollovers on slopes are the leading cause of serious injuries. This section is worth reading even if you skip everything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Know your slope limits.</strong> Most residential lawn tractors are rated for slopes up to roughly 15 degrees, and zero-turns are generally rated lower or advised against slopes entirely. Fifteen degrees is steeper than it sounds and less steep than most people assume their yard is. If you are unsure, a slope that is difficult to walk up comfortably is one to be very cautious about mowing on a rider.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Direction matters on a slope.</strong> With a lawn tractor, mow up and down slopes rather than across them, because a tractor is far more likely to tip sideways than to flip end over end. With a zero-turn, the guidance often reverses to mowing across slopes, and manufacturers differ, so read the manual for your specific machine rather than applying a general rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Zero-turns lose braking on downhill grades.</strong> A zero-turn slows and stops by resisting the drive wheels, and on a downhill slope with wet grass those wheels can simply slide, at which point steering is also gone. This is the specific failure mode that makes zero-turns unsuitable for hills, and it happens faster than most operators can react to.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never carry passengers.</strong> Riding mowers have no passenger seat, no restraints, and a rotating blade directly beneath. Children riding along with an adult is a recurring cause of severe injuries, most often when the child falls and the operator backs over them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Look behind before reversing, every time.</strong> Most riding mowers disable the blades in reverse by default for exactly this reason, and defeating that feature should be reserved for situations where you have confirmed the area is clear. Take the extra second to turn and look rather than trusting a mirror or a memory of what was there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Riding Mower Maintenance and Ownership Costs -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Riding Mower Maintenance and Ownership Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Riding mowers cost more to own than walk-behinds, and budgeting for that up front prevents unpleasant surprises in year three.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Annual service basics.</strong> Change the engine oil and filter once a season or per the hour interval in the manual, replace the air filter, replace the spark plugs, sharpen or replace the blades, and check tire pressures. Uneven tire pressure is a surprisingly common cause of an uneven cut, since it changes deck height side to side.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Grease the fittings.</strong> Riding mowers have grease points on spindles, front axle pivots, and steering components that most owners never touch. Ten minutes with a grease gun a couple of times a season prevents the bearing and bushing wear that produces expensive repairs later.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Level the deck annually.</strong> Deck level drifts over time and is the most common cause of a stepped or uneven cut. The procedure is in every manual, takes fifteen minutes with a tape measure on a flat surface, and improves cut appearance more than most owners expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery and fuel over winter.</strong> Remove or tender the battery over winter, since a discharged lead-acid battery left in the cold often will not recover. Stabilize the fuel or run the tank dry, because ethanol-blended gasoline gumming a carburetor is the leading cause of a mower that will not start in spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Budget realistically.</strong> Fuel, oil, filters, blades, and belts for a residential rider typically run a couple hundred dollars a year, plus an occasional larger repair. Electric riders eliminate most of that, which is a meaningful part of their long-term value case even though it never shows up on a price tag comparison.
          </p>
        </div>

