              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best riding lawn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most properties between one and three acres, the John Deere S120 offers the best combination of build quality, cut quality, and long-term serviceability. For an obstacle-heavy but relatively flat lawn, the Toro TimeCutter 42" zero-turn cuts mowing time by roughly a third. For an electric option, the Ryobi 80V HP Zero-Turn is the first we would genuinely recommend.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a lawn tractor or a zero-turn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy a zero-turn if your lawn is relatively flat but broken up by trees, beds, and structures, since it pivots around obstacles and saves substantial time. Buy a lawn tractor if your property has meaningful slope, since zero-turns can lose traction and slide on grade, or if you want to tow carts, spreaders, or a snow blade.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size deck do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 42-inch deck suits roughly half an acre to two acres and fits through most gates. A 46 to 48-inch deck is worthwhile on two to four acres. Above three acres, 54 inches and up is genuinely faster on open ground but leaves more trimming on properties with many obstacles. Always measure your gates and storage before choosing a width.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How steep a slope can a riding mower handle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential lawn tractors are rated for slopes up to about 15 degrees, and zero-turns are typically rated lower or advised against slopes entirely because their rear drive wheels can lose traction and slide on a downhill grade. Check your specific manual, and treat any slope that is uncomfortable to walk up as one to avoid on a rider.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric riding mowers worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your property fits within the roughly 2.5 acre range of a machine like the Ryobi 80V HP, yes. You eliminate fuel, oil changes, air filters, spark plugs, carburetor problems, and winterization entirely, and gain near-silent operation. The trade-offs are a higher purchase price and recharge time measured in hours rather than minutes at a fuel can.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do riding lawn mowers last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A budget rider typically gives five to eight seasons of residential use, while a well-maintained premium tractor with a Kawasaki or Kohler engine and a fabricated deck commonly runs fifteen years or more. Engine brand, deck construction, and whether the spindles are greaseable are the strongest predictors of longevity.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Riding Mower Buying Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Riding mowers are the most expensive lawn purchase most homeowners make, and the mistakes are correspondingly costly.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Zero-Turn for a Hilly Property</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Zero-turns are faster and more fun on flat ground, which is why people buy them for yards that do not suit them. On a downhill grade, the drive wheels that provide both propulsion and braking can lose traction and slide, taking steering with them. If your property has real slope, buy a tractor and accept the extra trimming.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Measuring the Gate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 42-inch deck can measure well over 50 inches wide overall including the discharge chute, and plenty of buyers discover after delivery that the machine will not fit through their fence gate or shed door. Measure every opening the mower must pass through before ordering anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Horsepower Over Deck Construction</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buyers compare engine horsepower closely and ignore whether the deck is stamped or fabricated, which is backwards. A fabricated welded deck resists damage and holds its shape for many more years, and cut quality degrades as a deck loses shape. Provided the engine has reserve capacity for the deck width, deck construction predicts satisfaction better.
              </p>
